  <h2>Choose Shipping From the Required Delivery Date</h2>
  <p>The best shipping method depends on order size, launch deadline, destination and acceptable landed cost. Buyers should plan from the required arrival date backwards, allowing time for final inspection, export handling, transit, customs clearance and local delivery.</p>
  <p>A low freight quotation is not useful if it excludes destination charges or arrives after the collection launch. Compare methods using the same carton volume, weight, Incoterm and delivery address.</p>

  <h2>Express, Air Freight and Sea Freight Compared</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Shipping method</th><th>Best for</th><th>Main watch point</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Express courier</td><td>Samples, urgent replacements and small launch quantities</td><td>Fast but expensive per kilogram; dimensional weight may apply</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Air freight</td><td>Medium orders with a firm retail deadline</td><td>Airport handling, customs and local delivery may be separate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sea freight</td><td>Large or less urgent orders where cost matters</td><td>Longer transit, port charges and greater planning buffer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DDP service</td><td>Buyers who want delivery with duty and customs responsibility included</td><td>Confirm exactly which destination charges and delivery services are covered</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Understand Incoterms Before Comparing Prices</h2>
  <p>Incoterms define where cost and responsibility move from seller to buyer. EXW, FOB, CIF and DDP quotations can look very different because they include different transport stages. Ask every supplier or forwarder to state the Incoterm, named place and excluded charges in writing.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>EXW:</strong> the buyer controls collection from the factory and most transport responsibility.</li>
    <li><strong>FOB:</strong> the supplier normally delivers export-cleared cargo to the named port.</li>
    <li><strong>CIF:</strong> ocean freight and insurance reach the named destination port, but destination costs remain.</li>
    <li><strong>DDP:</strong> the seller or logistics provider manages delivery with import duty responsibility included.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Carton Data Controls the Freight Estimate</h2>
  <p>Freight is calculated from actual weight, dimensional weight or shipment volume. Final estimates require carton count, carton dimensions, gross weight and delivery postcode. Packaging changes can therefore affect shipping cost even when the garment quantity stays the same.</p>
  <p>Ask for provisional carton assumptions during quotation and final carton data after packing. This reduces surprises between the first freight estimate and shipment booking.</p>

  <h2>Documents Commonly Required for Activewear Shipments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Commercial invoice with buyer, seller, product value and currency.</li>
    <li>Packing list with quantity, carton count, weights and dimensions.</li>
    <li>Product description and HS code for customs classification.</li>
    <li>Country-of-origin or other documents when required by the destination.</li>
    <li>Transport document such as airway bill or bill of lading.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Build a Realistic Delivery Timeline</h2>
  <p>Transit time is only one part of delivery. Include booking availability, export handling, port or airport movement, customs inspection and last-mile delivery. Peak seasons, holidays and weather can add uncertainty, so avoid promising a launch based on the fastest quoted transit time.</p>

  <h2>Activewear Shipping Checklist Before Cargo Release</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Confirm final inspection and production quantity.</li>
    <li>Verify carton marks, size assortment and packing list.</li>
    <li>Compare shipping methods using final weight and volume.</li>
    <li>Confirm Incoterm, customs responsibility and excluded charges.</li>
    <li>Check invoice, HS code and destination delivery details.</li>
  </ul>

